Part 3 – Core Concepts
3.1 Definition of a Boundary Pipe
A boundary pipe is defined as a pipe that:
•	Connects two different zones, OR
•	Connects a zone to an unassigned area (if enabled)
Example Scenarios
Scenario	Boundary?
Pipe between Zone A and Zone B	Yes
Pipe within Zone A	No
Pipe between Zone A and outside area	Optional (depends on settings)
________________________________________
3.2 Open vs. Closed Status
Open Boundary (Green)
•	Flow permitted between zones
•	Hydraulic continuity maintained
Closed Boundary (Red)
•	No flow between zones
•	Hydraulic isolation created
Closing all boundary pipes between two zones results in full hydraulic separation.
________________________________________
3.3 Boundary Control Methods
3.3.1 Manual Control (Interactive Mode)
Users manually toggle boundary pipes.
Procedure:
1.	Enable Interactive Links Mode
2.	Hover over a pipe to view details
3.	Click:
o	Green pipe → becomes Red (closed)
o	Red pipe → becomes Green (open)
o	Dark blue pipe → attribute popup (not toggleable)
Dashboard statistics update automatically.
________________________________________
3.3.2 Automatic Control (Rule-Based)
Users define selection rules in the left panel:
1.	Define boundary criteria
2.	Choose number of open connections per zone pair
3.	Select prioritization attribute
4.	Choose Highest or Lowest values
The system automatically selects which pipes remain open.
________________________________________
3.3.3 Combined Approach
Automatic baseline selection may be manually overridden. Manual changes persist unless reset is selected.
